/* ===== Graduate: the debates scholars still fight about ===== */
U({id:'gra',track:'gr',title:'Capital in depth',scene:'books',blurb:'Crisis theory, the falling rate of profit and what survives the critiques.',lessons:[
{t:'Accumulation and crisis',sub:'Why Marx thought capitalism destabilizes itself',items:[
 ["Marx's general formula for capital is **M–C–M′**: money buys commodities (labor power and means of production) in order to end with more money. Unlike C–M–C (selling in order to buy), this circuit has no natural end point.",
  M("What distinguishes M–C–M′ from C–M–C?",["Its aim is more money, without limit","It begins and ends with a different commodity","It involves no exchange","It ends in consumption"],"C–M–C ends when a need is met. M–C–M′ is restarted the moment it finishes, which Marx reads as a built-in drive to accumulate.")],
 ["The **organic composition of capital** is the ratio of constant to variable capital (c/v). Competition pushes firms to mechanize, raising it over time.",
  CASE("A firm advances c = 80 and v = 20, with a rate of surplus value of 100 percent. It then mechanizes to c = 90 and v = 10, and the rate of surplus value stays at 100 percent.",M("What happens to its rate of profit, s/(c+v)?",["It falls from 20% to 10%","It rises from 10% to 20%","It stays at 20%","It falls from 100% to 50%"],"With s/v fixed, s drops from 20 to 10 while total capital stays 100. This is the bare arithmetic of Marx's law."))],
 ["Hence the **law of the tendency of the rate of profit to fall** (LTRPF). Marx called it a tendency because of **counteracting factors**.",
  PICK("Which did Marx list as counteracting factors to the falling rate of profit?",["A rising rate of exploitation","Cheapening of machines and materials","Foreign trade"],["A rising organic composition of capital","Falling labor productivity"],"A rising organic composition is the cause of the tendency, not a counterweight to it.")],
 ["**Okishio's theorem** (1961) is the main formal objection: if real wages stay constant, a cost-reducing technique that a capitalist would rationally adopt cannot lower the general rate of profit. It raises it.",
  TF("Okishio's theorem shows that profit-raising innovations must lower the economy-wide profit rate when real wages are fixed.",false,"It shows the opposite. Defenders of Marx respond by challenging its assumptions: for example, the 'temporal single-system' school rejects its simultaneous valuation of inputs and outputs.")],
 ["Marxists disagree among themselves about what causes crises.",
  PAIR("Match the crisis theory to its mechanism.",[["Falling rate of profit","Mechanization erodes the profit rate"],["Underconsumption","Wages too low to buy the output"],["Profit squeeze","Tight labor markets raise wages"],["Disproportionality","Sectors grow out of balance"]],"Underconsumptionists and profit-squeeze theorists reach opposite policy conclusions about wages.")],
 ["Marx also argued accumulation maintains a **reserve army of labor**: a pool of unemployed that disciplines wages. When it shrinks, wages rise, profits are squeezed, investment slows and the pool refills.",
  ORD("Put the profit-squeeze cycle in order.",["Rapid accumulation absorbs the unemployed","Wages rise as labor grows scarce","Profits fall and investment slows","Layoffs rebuild the reserve army"],"Richard Goodwin formalized this as a predator-prey model in 1967.")],
 ["Empirical tests are contested because Marxian categories do not map neatly onto national accounts. Studies of the U.S. find a falling profit rate from the 1960s to about 1982 and a partial recovery after, with authors disputing the cause.",
  CASE("A researcher finds U.S. corporate profit rates fell from 1966 to 1982, a period of low unemployment, strong unions and rising real wages, then recovered as unions weakened.",M("Which crisis theory does this pattern most directly support?",["Profit squeeze","Underconsumption","Disproportionality","Okishio's theorem"],"Glyn and Sutcliffe made this argument for Britain, and Weisskopf for the U.S. Brenner instead blamed international overcapacity."))],
 ["The stakes: if the LTRPF holds, capitalism's crises are structural and no reform can abolish them. If it fails, crises may be manageable by policy, which is roughly the social democratic view.",
  EX("State the LTRPF argument and Okishio's objection in your own words.","Marx: competition forces firms to replace workers with machines. Since only living labor creates surplus value, a rising ratio of machinery to labor means surplus grows more slowly than total capital, so the rate of profit tends to fall. Okishio: capitalists only adopt techniques that cut costs at current prices, and if real wages do not rise, such techniques raise the general rate of profit once prices adjust. So mechanization alone cannot explain a falling profit rate. Something else, like rising wages, must be doing the work.")]
]},
{t:'Value after Sraffa',sub:'Is the labor theory redundant?',items:[
 ["**Piero Sraffa**'s *Production of Commodities by Means of Commodities* (1960) showed that relative prices and the profit rate can be derived directly from physical input-output data plus the real wage.",
  M("What did Sraffa's system determine prices from?",["Physical production data and the wage","Consumers' marginal utilities and their budgets","Labor values alone","The quantity of money"],"It was aimed at neoclassical capital theory, but it cut against Marx too.")],
 ["**Ian Steedman** (*Marx after Sraffa*, 1977) drew the conclusion: labor values are **redundant**. Anything they explain, the physical data explain directly, and with joint production values can even turn negative.",
  CASE("An analyst computes prices and the profit rate straight from the physical quantities of inputs and outputs and the real wage, never calculating labor values at all.",M("Whose critique of Marx does this procedure embody?",["Steedman's","Böhm-Bawerk's","Hayek's","Kornai's"],"This is the 'neo-Ricardian' critique."))],
 ["Responses came in three kinds. The **'New Interpretation'** (Duménil, Foley) redefines the value of labor power as the wage share of net output. The **TSSI** insists on sequential, non-dual valuation. **Value-form theorists** say value is about money and social form, not embodied hours.",
  PAIR("Match the response to its move.",[["New Interpretation","Value of labor power = wage share of net product"],["TSSI","Inputs valued at historical, not replacement, cost"],["Value-form theory","Value exists only through money and exchange"],["Analytical Marxism","Drop the labor theory altogether"]],"Each rescues something different, and each has been accused of changing the subject.")],
 ["The **Fundamental Marxian Theorem** (Okishio, Morishima): profits are positive if and only if surplus labor is positive. Critics noted the same theorem can be proven for any basic commodity: a 'steel theory of value.'",
  TF("The 'generalized commodity exploitation theorem' shows labor is uniquely exploitable among inputs.",false,"It shows the formal result holds for any basic input, such as steel or corn. So the theorem cannot by itself justify singling out labor. That needs a separate normative or sociological argument.")],
 ["**G. A. Cohen** argued the moral charge of exploitation does not need the labor theory: workers create the *product* that has value, and capitalists receive part of it without producing. That is enough.",
  SORT("Does this claim depend on the labor theory of value?",{"Depends on it":["Prices are regulated by labor time","The profit rate falls as c/v rises"],"Independent of it":["Owners receive income without producing","Workers lack access to means of production","Ownership is unequally distributed"]},"Cohen called this the 'plain argument' for exploitation.")],
 ["Roemer went further: the wage relation is not necessary for exploitation. In his models, people with unequal asset holdings who merely trade goods, or borrow and lend, end up in an exploitation relation.",
  CASE("In a model economy there is no labor market. Asset-poor producers borrow capital from asset-rich ones at interest and work for themselves. The poor work longer than socially necessary; the rich work less.",M("What does Roemer conclude from models like this?",["Unequal ownership, not wage labor, is the root","Exploitation needs a factory and a wage contract to occur","Interest is not exploitative","Markets remove exploitation"],"He calls it the 'isomorphism' of the credit-market and labor-market islands."))],
 ["But if exploitation is just a symptom of unequal assets, why care about it separately? Roemer bit the bullet and asked 'Should Marxists be interested in exploitation?' His answer: only derivatively.",
  PICK("Which positions did Roemer defend?",["Exploitation stems from unequal asset ownership","The real injustice is the unequal distribution itself"],["Labor time uniquely determines price","Exploitation requires direct coercion at work"],"His critics, such as Erik Olin Wright, replied that domination at the point of production matters in its own right.")],
 ["So the field has fragmented: some keep value theory as a theory of money and form, some keep exploitation without value theory, and some drop both for egalitarian political philosophy.",
  EX("Explain the 'redundancy' critique of the labor theory of value and one line of reply.","Sraffa showed prices and the profit rate can be calculated from physical input-output data and the real wage. Steedman concluded that labor values add nothing: they are a detour, and sometimes give paradoxical results. One reply (value-form theory) is that Marx was not offering a price calculator; value theory explains why labor takes the social form of money and capital at all. Another (the New Interpretation) redefines the key terms so that Marx's aggregate equalities hold by construction.")]
]}]});

U({id:'grb',track:'gr',title:'State theory and ideology',scene:'peaks',blurb:'Relative autonomy, ideological apparatuses and the limits of reform.',lessons:[
{t:'The Miliband–Poulantzas debate',sub:'Instrument, structure or arena',items:[
 ["**Ralph Miliband**'s *The State in Capitalist Society* (1969) took on pluralism empirically: state elites are drawn from, educated with and tied to the capitalist class.",
  M("What kind of evidence did Miliband emphasize?",["The social origins and ties of state elites","Mathematical models of prices and profit rates over time","Opinion polls of workers","Archaeological records"],"Hence the label 'instrumentalism': the state as an instrument in the hands of a class.")],
 ["**Nicos Poulantzas** replied that this concedes too much to the pluralists' method. The state's class character is **objective**: it follows from its function in reproducing the social formation, whoever staffs it.",
  CASE("A government of former trade unionists, with no ties to business, still cuts corporate taxes to prevent firms relocating abroad.",M("Whose theory handles this case more easily?",["Poulantzas's","Miliband's","Kropotkin's","Bernstein's"],"If personnel explained policy, changing the personnel should change the policy. A structural account predicts it will not."))],
 ["Poulantzas's key concept is the **relative autonomy** of the state. To secure capitalism's long-run interest, the state must be able to act against the short-run interests of particular capitalists.",
  PICK("Which state actions illustrate 'relative autonomy'?",["Factory Acts limiting the working day","Breaking up a monopoly","Bailing out banks against public opinion"],["A minister taking a bribe","A firm lobbying for a tax break"],"Marx's own chapter on the Factory Acts is the classic case: the state restrained individual capitalists to keep the working class alive.")],
 ["**Fred Block** ('The Ruling Class Does Not Rule,' 1977) gave the mechanism without functionalism: state managers pursue their own interest in revenue and re-election, both of which depend on **business confidence**.",
  ORD("Put Block's mechanism in order.",["A government proposes anti-business reform","Investors lose confidence and cut investment","Growth and tax revenue fall","State managers retreat to restore confidence"],"No conspiracy or class-conscious ruling class is required.")],
 ["In his last book Poulantzas moved toward seeing the state as a 'material condensation of a relationship of forces': a **terrain of struggle**, not a thing. He argued for a **democratic road** combining parliament with popular movements.",
  TF("The late Poulantzas held that the capitalist state must be smashed from outside and has no internal contradictions the left can use.",false,"He rejected Lenin's 'dual power' model and argued struggles run through the state itself. This was the theoretical basis of 'Eurocommunism' on its left wing.")],
 ["**Claus Offe** added that the welfare state is caught in a contradiction: capitalism cannot live with it (taxes and labor protections cut into profit) and cannot live without it (it secures legitimacy and reproduces labor).",
  SORT("Accumulation function or legitimation function of the state?",{"Accumulation":["Subsidizing infrastructure","Protecting property rights","Guaranteeing bank deposits"],"Legitimation":["Unemployment insurance","Public pensions"]},"James O'Connor's *The Fiscal Crisis of the State* (1973) argued the two functions pull the budget apart.")],
 ["**Adam Przeworski** modeled the electoral dilemma: workers are never an electoral majority, so socialist parties must seek allies, and to do so must dilute class appeals, weakening their hold on workers.",
  CASE("A socialist party gets 35 percent with a pure working-class program. To win it courts white-collar and small-business voters with a moderated platform. It wins office and loses some core workers to abstention and rivals.",M("What does Przeworski call this situation?",["The electoral dilemma of social democracy","The transformation problem of values into prices","The knowledge problem","The mass line"],"In *Capitalism and Social Democracy* (1985) he argues reformism was a rational response to this, not a betrayal."))],
 ["The combined upshot is sobering for the parliamentary road: structural dependence limits what can be done in office, and the electoral dilemma limits what can be promised to get there.",
  EX("Summarize the difference between instrumentalist and structuralist theories of the state, and why Block's account is sometimes seen as improving on both.","Instrumentalists (Miliband) explain pro-capitalist policy by who runs the state: elites with business backgrounds and ties. Structuralists (Poulantzas) say the state serves capital because of its role in the system, whoever staffs it, and needs relative autonomy to do so. Block supplies a concrete mechanism that needs neither a ruling-class conspiracy nor functionalism: state managers depend on tax revenue and re-election, both depend on investment, so they must maintain business confidence.")]
]},
{t:'Ideology and subjects',sub:'Althusser, the Frankfurt School and after',items:[
 ["**Louis Althusser** distinguished the **Repressive State Apparatus** (army, police, courts) from **Ideological State Apparatuses** (schools, churches, family, media), which function chiefly by ideology.",
  SORT("RSA or ISA, in Althusser's terms?",{"Repressive State Apparatus":["The police","The prison system"],"Ideological State Apparatuses":["The school system","Churches","The family"]},"He thought the school had replaced the church as the dominant ISA.")],
 ["For Althusser ideology is not false ideas. It is the 'imaginary relation' people live toward their real conditions, embedded in practices. It **interpellates** individuals as subjects.",
  CASE("A police officer shouts 'Hey, you there!' and a passer-by turns around, recognizing that the call was meant for them.",M("What does Althusser use this scene to illustrate?",["Interpellation","Surplus value","Relative autonomy","The reserve army"],"In turning, the individual accepts the position of subject addressed by authority. His critics found this left no room for resistance."))],
 ["He also argued for Marx's **epistemological break**: the mature Marx of *Capital* is a scientist of structures who abandoned the humanism of the early writings on alienation.",
  TF("Althusser celebrated the young Marx's writings on alienation as the true core of Marxism.",false,"He treated them as pre-scientific. Humanist Marxists such as E. P. Thompson attacked him fiercely for it in *The Poverty of Theory*.")],
 ["The **Frankfurt School** (Adorno, Horkheimer, Marcuse) asked why workers in advanced capitalism embraced it. Their answer stressed the **culture industry**, which sells standardized pleasure and absorbs dissent.",
  M("What did Adorno and Horkheimer mean by the 'culture industry'?",["Mass culture produced like any commodity","State censorship of the arts and of the daily press","Folk culture made by workers","Government funding of museums"],"Marcuse's *One-Dimensional Man* (1964) extended it: affluent society creates 'false needs' that bind people to the system.")],
 ["**Georg Lukács** had earlier theorized **reification**: under commodity production, social relations take on the appearance of thing-like, natural facts, and this shapes consciousness across society.",
  PAIR("Match the concept to its author.",[["Reification","Lukács"],["Interpellation","Althusser"],["Culture industry","Adorno and Horkheimer"],["Hegemony","Gramsci"]],"All four are attempts to explain consent, a problem classical Marxism had barely posed.")],
 ["The standing objection to all 'dominant ideology' theories (Abercrombie, Hill and Turner, 1980): subordinate classes often do *not* believe the dominant ideology. Order rests on what Marx called 'the dull compulsion of economic relations.'",
  CASE("Surveys show most workers in a country think the economy is rigged for the rich. They keep going to work and do not rebel.",M("Which explanation fits this best?",["The dull compulsion of needing a wage","Successful ideological indoctrination","Interpellation by the school","The culture industry's false needs"],"People can see through a system and still have no feasible exit from it."))],
 ["**Post-Marxists** such as Laclau and Mouffe (*Hegemony and Socialist Strategy*, 1985) went further: class has no privileged status. Political identities are constructed through discourse, and the left must link diverse struggles.",
  PICK("Which claims belong to Laclau and Mouffe's post-Marxism?",["Class interests are not given prior to politics","The left must articulate plural struggles"],["The economy determines politics in the last instance","The proletariat is the universal class"],"Critics such as Ellen Meiksins Wood called this a 'retreat from class.'")],
 ["The debate matters for strategy: if consent is produced by ideology, the task is cultural. If compliance is produced by dependence on the wage, the task is to build material alternatives and organization.",
  EX("Why does it matter politically whether workers' compliance is explained by ideology or by 'dull compulsion'?","If workers accept capitalism because they have absorbed its ideas, then change requires a long cultural struggle to shift common sense, through media, education and intellectual work. If they comply mainly because they need a wage and see no workable alternative, then arguments will not be enough: what is needed is organization and institutions, such as unions, parties and decommodified services, that lower the cost of resisting and make alternatives credible.")]
]}]});

U({id:'grc',track:'gr',title:'Planning, markets and models',scene:'coop',blurb:'From Lange to Kornai to participatory planning: can socialism calculate?',lessons:[
{t:'The debate, second round',sub:'Information, incentives and innovation',items:[
 ["The standard textbook story was that Lange won the 1930s debate. From the 1980s, scholars like Don Lavoie argued the Austrians had been misread: their point concerned **dynamic** discovery under uncertainty, not solving static equations.",
  M("According to Lavoie's revision, what had Lange's model failed to address?",["Entrepreneurial discovery under uncertainty","The existence of consumer goods and personal property","How to add up labor hours","Whether money should exist"],"Lange's planners find prices for a given list of goods and techniques. Hayek asked where the list comes from.")],
 ["Hayek stressed **tacit knowledge**: know-how that cannot be fully written down or transmitted to a center (a concept later developed by Michael Polanyi).",
  SORT("Could this knowledge be reported to a planning board?",{"Codifiable in principle":["Last month's steel output","The number of machines in a plant"],"Tacit or fleeting":["A foreman's feel for a temperamental machine","A trader's hunch about local demand","A half-formed idea for a new product"]},"The tacit-knowledge argument is why Hayekians are unmoved by bigger computers.")],
 ["Mainstream information economics added the **incentive** problem: agents have private information and may misreport it. The Soviet 'ratchet effect' is a textbook case.",
  CASE("A plant manager knows the factory could produce 120 units. If she reports that, next year's target will be 130. She reports a capacity of 100.",M("What general problem is this?",["Incentive incompatibility","Commodity fetishism","Underconsumption","Interpellation"],"Leonid Hurwicz's mechanism design theory grew partly out of the socialist calculation debate."))],
 ["**Kornai**'s *Economics of Shortage* (1980) explained why classical socialism was a **resource-constrained** system with chronic shortage, while capitalism is **demand-constrained** with chronic slack.",
  PAIR("Match Kornai's concept to its meaning.",[["Soft budget constraint","Losses are covered by the state"],["Investment hunger","Firms always want more capital"],["Shortage economy","Sellers' markets and queues are normal"],["Paternalism","The state treats firms as dependents"]],"He argued these traits form a coherent system and cannot be reformed away one at a time.")],
 ["Kornai concluded that market socialism under a ruling Communist Party is unstable: as long as the state owns the firms and the party controls appointments, budget constraints stay soft.",
  TF("Kornai believed market-socialist reforms such as Hungary's in 1968 had hardened budget constraints successfully.",false,"He judged that they had not. Bailouts continued because the state as owner could not credibly commit to letting firms fail.")],
 ["On the other side, **Cockshott and Cottrell** (*Towards a New Socialism*, 1993) argue labor-time calculation of millions of products is computationally tractable with modern methods, and consumer goods markets can signal demand.",
  PICK("Which are objections commonly raised against computerized central planning?",["The data must first be honestly reported","Tacit knowledge is never entered","It says little about innovation"],["Computers cannot multiply large matrices","Consumers have no preferences"],"The computation itself is the least of the problems. Their defenders point to how much planning already occurs inside firms such as Amazon.")],
 ["Chile's **Project Cybersyn** (1971 to 1973), designed with the cyberneticist Stafford Beer, linked nationalized factories by telex to a central operations room. It was used to route trucks during the 1972 owners' strike.",
  ORD("Put these episodes of planning thought in order.",["Mises's 1920 essay","Lange's trial-and-error model","Hayek's 'Use of Knowledge in Society'","Project Cybersyn in Chile"],"1920, 1936 to 1937, 1945 and 1971 to 1973.")],
 ["Where the debate stands: most agree comprehensive physical planning failed. Disagreement is over whether the cause was planning as such, authoritarian politics, primitive technology, or the lack of any exit for failing firms.",
  EX("Explain why Hayekians say more computing power does not answer their critique.","Their critique is not that planners lack the arithmetic capacity to solve equations. It is that the data for those equations do not exist in a form anyone could enter. Much relevant knowledge is local, tacit and constantly changing, and some of it is only discovered through rivalrous competition, when entrepreneurs try things and learn from profit and loss. A computer can process reported data but cannot generate knowledge nobody has yet, or extract know-how people cannot articulate or have reason to hide.")]
]},
{t:'Models of a feasible socialism',sub:'Nove, Roemer, Schweickart, Albert and Hahnel',items:[
 ["**Alec Nove**'s *The Economics of Feasible Socialism* (1983) defined 'feasible' as achievable within a generation without assuming abundance or a new human nature. He proposed a mix of state firms, co-ops, small private business and markets.",
  M("What did Nove mean by 'feasible' socialism?",["Achievable soon without assuming abundance","Possible only after a successful world revolution","A fully moneyless economy","Requiring no state at all"],"He dismissed moneyless planning as a fantasy that Marx's vagueness had encouraged.")],
 ["**Roemer**'s coupon socialism: every adult gets an equal, non-cashable endowment of coupons to buy shares in firms. Shares pay dividends but cannot be sold for money or bequeathed.",
  CASE("At 18 each citizen receives coupons, invests them in mutual funds holding company shares, and draws dividends for life. At death the portfolio returns to the public treasury.",M("What is this design meant to achieve?",["Equal profit income without central planning","Direct worker control of every firm by its own council","Abolition of all markets","A return to barter"],"It equalizes capital income and keeps a stock market to discipline managers. Critics note it leaves workplace hierarchy untouched."))],
 ["**David Schweickart**'s Economic Democracy has three parts: worker self-managed firms, a market for goods, and **social control of investment** through a capital-assets tax distributed via public banks.",
  PICK("Which are components of Schweickart's Economic Democracy?",["Worker-managed firms","Markets for goods and services","Public control of new investment"],["A private stock market","Central setting of all prices"],"It is modeled partly on Mondragon and Yugoslavia, with investment socialized to avoid Yugoslavia's problems.")],
 ["Labor-managed firms raise a classic theoretical worry (Benjamin Ward, 1958): a firm maximizing income *per member* may respond to higher demand by restricting membership instead of expanding.",
  TF("Ward's model predicts worker-managed firms will always hire more aggressively than capitalist firms when prices rise.",false,"It predicts the opposite, a 'perverse' supply response. Empirical studies of real co-ops find little evidence of it, but co-ops do tend to adjust pay instead of employment in downturns.")],
 ["**Albert and Hahnel**'s **participatory economics** (Parecon) rejects markets entirely: workers' and consumers' councils iterate proposals through facilitation boards. Jobs are 'balanced' for empowerment, and pay follows effort and sacrifice.",
  SORT("Market socialism or participatory economics?",{"Market socialism":["Firms compete for customers","Prices are set by supply and demand"],"Participatory economics":["Councils negotiate a plan iteratively","Balanced job complexes","Pay according to effort and sacrifice"]},"The old quip about such schemes, usually credited to Oscar Wilde, is that socialism would take too many evenings.")],
 ["The **1970s Yugoslav experience** is the main evidence on self-management: firms underinvested from retained earnings, borrowed heavily and pushed wages up, contributing to inflation and unemployment.",
  CASE("Workers at a self-managed firm can take this year's surplus as wages, or reinvest it. If they reinvest and later leave or retire, they have no claim on the firm's future earnings.",M("What problem does this create?",["A bias against reinvesting","Too much investment","Pressure to cut wages","A shortage of members"],"This is the 'horizon problem' analyzed by Furubotn and Pejovich. Mondragon addresses it with individual capital accounts paid out on departure."))],
 ["**Erik Olin Wright** proposed judging institutions by three criteria: **desirability**, **viability** and **achievability**, and steering by a 'socialist compass' toward more social power over the economy.",
  PAIR("Match Wright's criterion to its question.",[["Desirability","Would it be good if it worked?"],["Viability","Would it be stable and work as intended?"],["Achievability","Can we get there from here?"],["Real utopias","Existing institutions that prefigure alternatives"]],"His examples included participatory budgeting, Wikipedia and worker co-ops.")],
 ["A fair summary: each blueprint solves one failure of the Soviet model by reintroducing something its critics distrust (markets, a stock market, or endless meetings).",
  EX("Choose one model (coupon socialism, Economic Democracy or participatory economics) and give its main strength and main weakness.","Coupon socialism (Roemer): it would spread profit income equally while keeping markets and a stock market to discipline managers, so it handles efficiency concerns well; but it changes nothing about hierarchy at work. Economic Democracy (Schweickart): gives workers control of their firms and society control of investment; but co-ops may underinvest or resist hiring. Participatory economics: removes markets and their inequalities and gives everyone a say; but it demands heavy participation and it is unclear the planning process would converge or reward innovation.")]
]}]});

U({id:'grd',track:'gr',title:'Justice, equality and class today',scene:'city',blurb:'Analytical Marxism, the egalitarian turn and new maps of class.',lessons:[
{t:'Marxism meets political philosophy',sub:'Cohen, Rawls and the egalitarians',items:[
 ["**Analytical Marxism** (the 'September Group': Cohen, Roemer, Elster, Wright, Przeworski) set out to rebuild Marxist claims with the tools of analytic philosophy and rational choice theory.",
  M("What defined the method of the analytical Marxists?",["Analytic rigor and rational-choice tools","Dialectical logic and the philosophy of internal relations","Loyalty to the Soviet line","Psychoanalytic theory"],"They called themselves, half-jokingly, the 'No-Bullshit Marxism Group.'")],
 ["**Cohen**'s *Karl Marx's Theory of History: A Defence* (1978) reconstructed historical materialism as a **functional explanation**: production relations exist because they suit the development of the productive forces.",
  TF("Cohen defended the 'primacy of the productive forces' over the relations of production.",true,"On his reading, relations change when they come to fetter the forces. Critics such as Brenner argued class struggle, not technology, drove the transition from feudalism.")],
 ["Marx had dismissed talk of justice as ideology. Analytical Marxists concluded socialism *does* need a theory of justice, bringing them into dialogue with **John Rawls**.",
  CASE("Rawls's 'difference principle' allows inequalities only if they benefit the worst off. A talented surgeon says she will only work hard if paid ten times the average.",M("What was Cohen's objection to using the principle to justify her pay?",["Her demand is itself unjust if she accepts the principle","Surgeons do nothing that benefits the worst-off group in society","Incentives never affect effort","Rawls opposed all inequality"],"Cohen argued justice applies to personal choices and a society's ethos, not just the 'basic structure.' This is the 'incentives argument' in *Rescuing Justice and Equality*."))],
 ["Rawls himself held that welfare-state capitalism does *not* satisfy his principles. Only **property-owning democracy** or **liberal (market) socialism** could.",
  PICK("Which regimes did Rawls say could realize justice as fairness?",["Property-owning democracy","Liberal socialism"],["Welfare-state capitalism","Laissez-faire capitalism","Command-economy socialism"],"He thought welfare-state capitalism lets a small class control the means of production and so political life.")],
 ["Cohen's **camping trip** argument (*Why Not Socialism?*, 2009): on a camping trip nobody charges friends for using their fishing rod. We practice community and equality. Why should society be different?",
  SORT("Is this a question of desirability or of feasibility in Cohen's argument?",{"Desirability":["Is the camping-trip ethos attractive?","Is market motivation morally shabby?"],"Feasibility":["Can generosity scale to millions?","Can we coordinate without price signals?","Do we know how to design it?"]},"Cohen thought the principle was clearly desirable and the feasibility genuinely unknown: 'our problem is one of design.'")],
 ["**Luck egalitarianism** (Dworkin, Cohen, Arneson) says inequalities are unjust when they stem from unchosen circumstances, but not when they reflect genuine choices.",
  CASE("Two people have equal talents and opportunities. One works long hours and saves; the other prefers leisure. They end up with unequal wealth.",M("How does a luck egalitarian judge this inequality?",["Acceptable, since it reflects choice","Unjust, since any inequality is","Unjust, since effort is unchosen","Acceptable only if it helps the worst off"],"**Elizabeth Anderson** attacked this view as harsh to the imprudent and disrespectful to the unlucky, and proposed 'democratic equality' instead: equality of social standing."))],
 ["**Nancy Fraser** argued justice requires both **redistribution** (class) and **recognition** (status), and that the 'progressive neoliberalism' of the 1990s delivered the second as a substitute for the first.",
  PAIR("Match the theorist to the idea.",[["Rawls","The difference principle"],["Cohen","Justice applies to personal choices too"],["Anderson","Democratic (relational) equality"],["Fraser","Redistribution and recognition"]],"Fraser later added 'representation' as a third dimension.")],
 ["The turn to philosophy changed the question from 'Is socialism inevitable?' to 'Is it just, and is it feasible?'",
  EX("Explain Cohen's incentives objection to Rawls in your own words.","Rawls allows unequal pay if it is needed to motivate talented people to do work that benefits the worst off. Cohen points out that the inequality is 'needed' only because the talented choose to demand it. If they sincerely accepted the principle that inequalities must benefit the worst off, they would work without the extra pay. So the incentive is more like a ransom than a justification, and a just society needs an egalitarian ethos, not only just rules.")]
]},
{t:'Class in the twenty-first century',sub:'Piketty, Wright and the new debates',items:[
 ["**Erik Olin Wright** remapped class to account for managers and professionals: they occupy **contradictory class locations**, exploited as employees yet holding authority or scarce skills.",
  M("Why did Wright call middle-class positions 'contradictory locations'?",["They combine features of opposed classes","They change from one year to the next","They are outside the economy","They have no political views"],"A plant manager is paid a wage, yet controls others' labor.")],
 ["**Thomas Piketty**'s *Capital in the Twenty-First Century* (2013) argued that when the return on capital exceeds the growth rate (**r > g**), inherited wealth grows faster than income and inequality rises.",
  DATA("Top 10% share of national income, about 2020",[["Europe",36,"36%"],["United States",46,"46%"],["Latin America",55,"55%"],["Middle East",58,"58%"]],M("What does the chart suggest about inequality?",["It varies widely between regions","It is identical everywhere","It is lowest in the United States","It is highest in Europe"],"Piketty stresses that such differences show inequality is shaped by institutions and politics, not by technology alone."),"Source: World Inequality Report 2022")],
 ["Marxist critics (Harvey, among others) replied that Piketty treats capital as a stock of wealth, not a social relation, and so has no theory of why r stays above g. Mainstream critics noted much of the rise in the capital share is housing.",
  SORT("Who made this criticism of Piketty?",{"Marxist critics":["Capital is a relation, not a sum of assets","He ignores class struggle over the wage"],"Mainstream critics":["The rising capital share is mostly housing","Diminishing returns should push r down","r > g does not by itself imply rising inequality"]},"His later *Capital and Ideology* shifted the emphasis from r > g to politics and ideas.")],
 ["**David Harvey** revived Marx's 'primitive accumulation' as **accumulation by dispossession**: privatization, financialization and enclosure transfer existing wealth instead of creating new value.",
  PICK("Which are examples of 'accumulation by dispossession' in Harvey's sense?",["Privatizing a public water system","Enclosing common land","Foreclosing homes after predatory lending"],["Raising output with a new machine","Paying wages for factory work"],"New machinery and wage work are ordinary expanded reproduction.")],
 ["**Ecosocialists** argue capitalism's growth imperative collides with planetary limits. **John Bellamy Foster** reads Marx as theorizing a **metabolic rift** between society and nature. **Degrowth** writers (Hickel, Saito) call for planned reduction of material throughput in rich countries.",
  CASE("One thinker argues public investment and new technology can 'decouple' growth from emissions. Another argues rich countries must deliberately shrink energy and material use and share work.",M("What are these two positions usually called?",["Green growth and degrowth","Reform and revisionism","Mutualism and syndicalism","Monetarism and Keynesianism"],"Ecomodernist socialists such as Matt Huber side with the first and argue degrowth cannot win working-class support."))],
 ["The **platform economy** raises new classification fights: are app-based drivers employees or independent contractors? The answer determines minimum wage, bargaining rights and who bears risk.",
  TF("Classifying a driver as an independent contractor gives the driver the legal right to bargain collectively under U.S. labor law.",false,"U.S. labor law covers employees. Contractors are excluded, and collective action by them can even raise antitrust issues. That is why classification is fought over.")],
 ["**Universal basic income** divides the left. Supporters (van Parijs) see 'real freedom for all' and bargaining power to refuse bad jobs. Critics fear it subsidizes low wages and replaces public services.",
  PAIR("Match the proposal to its core idea.",[["Universal basic income","Unconditional cash to all"],["Job guarantee","Public employment for anyone who wants it"],["Universal basic services","Free provision of essentials"],["Social wealth fund","Collectively owned capital paying a dividend"]],"Each implies a different view of work and the state.")],
 ["Underneath runs an old question in new form: is the aim to redistribute the proceeds of capital (taxes, dividends), or to change who controls it?",
  EX("State Piketty's r > g argument and one criticism of it, in your own words.","If the rate of return on capital (r) is persistently higher than the economy's growth rate (g), wealth that is already owned grows faster than incomes from work. Since wealth is concentrated and inherited, the share held by the top rises over time unless wars, taxes or policy interrupt it. Criticisms: r > g alone does not imply rising inequality unless the rich save most of their returns; much of the measured rise in capital's share reflects housing prices; and Marxists add that treating capital as a pile of assets ignores the power relations that determine r in the first place.")]
]}]});

U({id:'gre',track:'gr',title:'Empire and the world system',scene:'world',blurb:'Imperialism, dependency and the socialism of the colonized.',lessons:[
{t:'Theories of imperialism',sub:'Hobson to Wallerstein',items:[
 ["**J. A. Hobson** (a liberal, 1902) explained imperialism by **underconsumption**: low wages at home left surplus savings seeking outlets abroad. His cure was redistribution at home.",
  M("What did Hobson think would remove the economic root of imperialism?",["Raising workers' incomes at home","A larger navy","Higher tariffs on imports","Abolishing the gold standard at once"],"Lenin borrowed Hobson's data and rejected his remedy: for Lenin imperialism was a necessary stage, not a policy.")],
 ["The Marxist classics disagreed. **Luxemburg** held capitalism needs non-capitalist markets to realize surplus value. **Hilferding** stressed the fusion of banks and industry. **Kautsky** floated 'ultra-imperialism': a peaceful cartel of great powers.",
  PAIR("Match the theorist to the thesis.",[["Luxemburg","Capital needs a non-capitalist outside"],["Hilferding","Finance capital fuses banks and industry"],["Kautsky","Great powers could form a peaceful cartel"],["Lenin","Rivalry among monopolies makes war inevitable"]],"Lenin attacked Kautsky bitterly. The post-1945 U.S.-led order looked more like Kautsky's picture than Lenin's.")],
 ["After 1945, **dependency theory** (Prebisch, Frank, Amin) argued underdevelopment was not a stage before development. It was produced by the way peripheral economies were integrated into the world market.",
  SRC("Underdevelopment is not due to the survival of archaic institutions... it is generated by the very same historical process which also generated economic development: the development of capitalism itself.","Andre Gunder Frank, The Development of Underdevelopment, 1966",M("What is Frank denying?",["That poor countries are simply 'behind'","That capitalism ever existed","That trade occurs between nations","That colonies had economies"],"He was attacking modernization theory, which pictured every society climbing the same ladder."))],
 ["The **Prebisch–Singer hypothesis**: the terms of trade of primary-commodity exporters tend to decline against manufactures over the long run.",
  CASE("In 1960 a country must export 10 tons of coffee to import one tractor. In 1990 it must export 25 tons for the same tractor.",M("What has happened to its terms of trade?",["They have deteriorated","They have improved","They are unchanged","They cannot be measured"],"This was the argument for **import-substitution industrialization**: build domestic industry behind tariffs."))],
 ["**Immanuel Wallerstein**'s **world-systems analysis** divides the capitalist world economy into **core**, **semi-periphery** and **periphery**, linked by unequal exchange.",
  SORT("Typical of core or periphery in world-systems analysis?",{"Core":["High-wage, capital-intensive production","Strong states","Control of finance"],"Periphery":["Raw-material exports","Low-wage, labor-intensive production"]},"The semi-periphery (for example, Brazil or South Korea in the 1970s) mixes both and stabilizes the system.")],
 ["The East Asian 'tigers' were dependency theory's hardest case: South Korea and Taiwan industrialized *through* export integration, with strong developmental states, land reform and U.S. Cold War backing.",
  TF("South Korea's industrialization is usually cited as confirming the strongest version of dependency theory.",false,"It is the standard counterexample, though its state-led methods (credit control, protection, planning) were far from free-market orthodoxy too.")],
 ["**Bill Warren** argued from within Marxism (*Imperialism: Pioneer of Capitalism*, 1980) that imperialism spreads capitalist development, as Marx had expected. **Emmanuel**'s 'unequal exchange' located exploitation in wage differences between nations.",
  PICK("Which claims would a dependency theorist accept?",["The periphery's poverty is produced by its links to the core","Delinking or protection may be needed to develop"],["All countries pass through the same stages","Free trade benefits core and periphery equally"],"The rejected pair are the tenets of modernization theory and orthodox trade theory.")],
 ["So Marxists have held nearly every possible view: that imperialism blocks development, that it spreads development, and that it is capitalism's condition of existence.",
  EX("Contrast modernization theory with dependency theory in your own words.","Modernization theory sees poor countries as at an earlier stage on a single path all societies travel; with investment, education and trade they will catch up. Dependency theory says poor countries are not behind but have been shaped by their role in the world economy as suppliers of raw materials and cheap labor to rich countries. Their underdevelopment and the core's development are two sides of one process, so deeper integration on the same terms will not close the gap.")]
]},
{t:'National liberation',sub:'Fanon, Cabral and the Third World project',items:[
 ["Lenin's Comintern backed anti-colonial movements. The Indian Marxist **M. N. Roy** argued with him in 1920 over whether Communists should ally with the 'national bourgeoisie' or organize workers and peasants independently.",
  M("What was at stake between Lenin and Roy in 1920?",["Alliances with nationalist elites in the colonies","Whether colonies should exist at all in the modern world","The price of grain in Russia","The date of the German revolution"],"The question returned disastrously in China in 1927, when the nationalist Kuomintang massacred its Communist allies.")],
 ["**Frantz Fanon**, a psychiatrist from Martinique who joined Algeria's FLN, argued in *The Wretched of the Earth* (1961) that colonialism is sustained by violence and that decolonization remakes the colonized.",
  CASE("After independence, a new elite takes over the colonizers' villas and offices, lives off its role as middleman to foreign firms, and bans opposition in the name of national unity.",M("What did Fanon call this danger?",["The pitfalls of national consciousness","The mass line","The metabolic rift","The epistemological break in Marx's work"],"He predicted the 'national bourgeoisie' would become a caricature of its former masters. Many think he was right."))],
 ["**Amílcar Cabral**, who led the independence struggle in Guinea-Bissau, argued the petty-bourgeois leadership of liberation movements must commit **'class suicide'**: abandon its class interests and merge with the people.",
  TF("Cabral believed the educated leadership of liberation movements would serve the people automatically once independence was won.",false,"He saw their class position as the main danger and called for them to renounce it. He was assassinated in 1973, months before independence.")],
 ["The 1955 **Bandung Conference** and the **Non-Aligned Movement** (1961: Nehru, Nasser, Tito, Sukarno, Nkrumah) sought a path outside both blocs. In 1974 the UN adopted a call for a **New International Economic Order**.",
  ORD("Put these in order.",["The Bandung Conference","The Non-Aligned Movement is founded","The UN declaration on a New International Economic Order","The Third World debt crisis"],"1955, 1961, 1974 and 1982.")],
 ["The **debt crisis** ended the project. After U.S. interest rates spiked in 1979 to 1981, indebted states turned to the IMF, which required **structural adjustment**: austerity, privatization, trade liberalization.",
  PICK("Which were typical conditions of 1980s structural adjustment programs?",["Cutting public spending","Privatizing state firms","Removing trade barriers"],["Nationalizing banks","Raising food subsidies"],"Vijay Prashad calls this the 'assassination' of the Third World project. Defenders say the state-led models were already failing.")],
 ["**African socialism** claimed precolonial communal roots (Nyerere: 'ujamaa,' familyhood). **Sankara** in Burkina Faso combined self-reliance with women's rights and anti-corruption, and ruled through a military government that repressed striking unions.",
  SORT("Sort these features of Sankara's government (1983 to 1987).",{"Praised":["Mass vaccination of children","Banning forced marriage","Cutting officials' privileges"],"Criticized":["Rule by military council","Repression of unions and opponents"]},"He was killed in a coup led by his deputy, Blaise Compaoré.")],
 ["In Latin America the 'pink tide' after 1998 (Chávez, Lula, Morales) used commodity booms to cut poverty sharply. Venezuela's collapse after 2014, under price controls, oil dependence, corruption and later sanctions, is the cautionary case.",
  DATA("Share of population in poverty, Bolivia",[["2005",59.6,"59.6%"],["2011",45.1,"45.1%"],["2018",34.6,"34.6%"]],M("What does this chart show for Bolivia under Evo Morales?",["Poverty fell substantially","Poverty rose every year","Poverty was unchanged","Poverty fell to zero"],"Bolivia nationalized gas and ran cautious budgets. Critics note its reliance on gas exports and Morales's bid to stay in office past term limits."),"Source: Bolivian national statistics (INE), national poverty line")],
 ["A recurring pattern: movements that united a nation against an empire found it far harder to transform an economy still dependent on that empire's markets and credit.",
  EX("In your own words, what did Fanon warn would happen after independence, and why?","He warned that the nationalist middle class that inherits the state has no real economic base of its own: it does not own industry or invent anything. So it steps into the colonizers' role as an intermediary for foreign capital, enriches itself, and uses the language of national unity, and eventually one-party rule, to silence the peasants and workers who actually won independence. Formal freedom arrives while economic dependence continues.")]
]}]});

U({id:'grf',track:'gr',title:'Weighing actually existing socialism',scene:'mill',blurb:'Numbers, counterfactuals and how historians argue about them.',lessons:[
{t:'Counting the costs',sub:'Archives, famines and terror',items:[
 ["Before 1991 estimates of Stalin-era repression varied wildly. Soviet archives gave harder numbers: about **680,000 to 750,000 executions** in 1937 to 1938, and roughly **1.5 to 1.7 million recorded deaths** in Gulag camps and colonies from 1930 to 1953.",
  DATA("Recorded executions for 'counter-revolutionary' crimes, USSR",[["1936",1.1,"about 1,100"],["1937",353,"about 353,000"],["1938",329,"about 329,000"],["1939",2.6,"about 2,600"]],M("What does the pattern show?",["A sudden, centrally switched campaign","A slow and steady increase over a decade","Random year-to-year noise","A decline through the decade"],"The Terror was turned on by NKVD Order 00447 in July 1937 and off by a Politburo resolution in November 1938."),"Source: NKVD records as published by Getty, Rittersporn and Zemskov (1993)")],
 ["Historians divide into a **totalitarian school** (Conquest, Pipes), stressing ideology and control from above, and **revisionists** (Fitzpatrick, Getty), stressing social support, chaos and initiative from below.",
  SORT("Totalitarian school or revisionist school?",{"Totalitarian school":["Terror flowed from the top by design","Ideology explains the system"],"Revisionists":["Local officials and denunciations drove much of it","Upward mobility created real supporters","The center was often poorly informed"]},"The archives gave each side something: quotas were set by Stalin personally, and local officials begged to raise them.")],
 ["On the 1932 to 1933 famine, the debate is over **intent**. Conquest and Snyder emphasize policies targeting Ukraine (blacklists, the January 1933 ban on leaving). Davies and Wheatcroft argue Stalin's policies were brutal and ideological but not designed to kill.",
  TF("Davies and Wheatcroft argue the Soviet leadership bears no responsibility for the 1932 to 1933 famine.",false,"They hold the leadership responsible for catastrophic policy and callous response. What they dispute is that mass death was the intended aim.")],
 ["*The Black Book of Communism* (1997) claimed up to 100 million deaths worldwide. Two of its own contributors objected that the editor inflated totals to reach a round number and equated famine deaths with executions.",
  PICK("Which criticisms have been made of the 'Black Book' death toll?",["It merges famine deaths with deliberate killings","Its editor's totals exceed his contributors' figures","It applies no similar accounting to capitalist states"],["It uses no archival material at all","It ignores China entirely"],"None of this makes the documented crimes smaller. It concerns what a single headline number can mean.")],
 ["For China's Great Leap, demographic reconstructions range from about **15 to 30 million** excess deaths (Ashton; Banister; official-data studies) to **45 million** (Dikötter). The spread reflects different baseline mortality assumptions.",
  CASE("Two demographers use the same census data. One assumes 'normal' mortality would have continued its 1950s decline; the other assumes the 1957 death rate would have held.",M("Why do their estimates of excess famine deaths differ?",["They compare against different baselines","They use different countries","One ignores the census","Neither counts children"],"'Excess deaths' is always a comparison with a counterfactual, so the baseline does much of the work."))],
 ["Sen and Drèze's comparison: China's famine killed tens of millions, yet India's higher *normal* mortality meant that 'every eight years or so more people die in India... than died in China in the gigantic famine.'",
  M("What point were Sen and Drèze making with this comparison?",["Chronic deprivation can kill on a famine's scale","Famines are not important compared with wars","India's system was socialist","China had a free press"],"They held both that democracy prevents famine and that it can tolerate quiet, endemic hunger.")],
 ["Comparative method matters. Judging Communist states only by their worst episodes and capitalist ones only by their averages, or the reverse, yields whatever conclusion one started with.",
  ORD("Put these steps for a fair comparative judgment in order.",["Define the outcome to compare","Choose comparable cases and periods","Specify the counterfactual baseline","Report the range of estimates"],"Most polemics skip the second and third steps.")],
 ["A defensible summary: the archives confirmed mass state violence beyond dispute, lowered some Cold War-era estimates, and left questions of intent and comparison open.",
  EX("Why do estimates of famine deaths differ so widely between serious scholars?","Because 'excess deaths' are not counted directly. They are calculated as the difference between deaths that occurred, which must themselves be reconstructed from imperfect censuses and registration, and deaths that would have occurred without the famine. Different assumptions about the normal death rate, about under-registration and about lost births produce very different totals from the same data.")]
]},
{t:'Counting the gains, and the exit',sub:'Welfare outcomes and the transition',items:[
 ["Robert Allen's *Farm to Factory* (2003) argued the USSR was among the most successful developing economies from 1928 to 1970, and that a continued NEP with planning could have achieved most of the growth without collectivization.",
  M("What is Allen's counterfactual claim?",["Most of the growth did not require collectivization","No growth at all was possible without mass terror","The tsarist economy would have grown faster","Growth figures were entirely invented"],"Critics such as Cheremukhin and co-authors model a tsarist-trend counterfactual and find Stalin's policies reduced welfare.")],
 ["On basic welfare, a 1986 study by Cereseto and Waitzkin found socialist countries had better physical quality of life (infant mortality, literacy, life expectancy) than capitalist countries **at the same income level**.",
  CASE("A study compares health and literacy across countries, but only within groups of similar GDP per person.",M("Why control for income level?",["To separate the system's effect from wealth's","To hide the real differences between the systems","Because rich countries have no data","To exclude all socialist countries"],"The finding is suggestive, not conclusive: data came from the states themselves, and political freedoms were not measured."))],
 ["Gains flattened later. Soviet life expectancy stagnated from the mid-1960s, and male life expectancy fell, with alcohol a major factor.",
  DATA("Male life expectancy at birth, Russia",[["1965",64.5],["1980",61.4],["1987",64.9],["1994",57.4]],PICK("Which statements does the chart support?",["It fell during the Brezhnev years","It rose during the 1985 to 1987 anti-alcohol campaign","It fell steeply in the early transition"],["It rose every decade","It was highest in 1994"],"The 1990s mortality crisis cost millions of premature deaths, as Stuckler and others have documented."),"Source: Human Mortality Database")],
 ["The **transition** debate set 'shock therapy' (Sachs, Åslund: move fast before opponents regroup) against **gradualism** (Stiglitz, Roland: build institutions first; China as the model).",
  SORT("Shock therapy or gradualism?",{"Shock therapy":["Free most prices at once","Privatize rapidly","Poland 1990, Russia 1992"],"Gradualism":["Dual-track pricing","Keep state firms while new ones grow"]},"Poland recovered within a few years. Russia's output fell for most of a decade. Starting conditions and institutions differed greatly.")],
 ["Branko Milanović's 'For Whom the Wall Fell?' (2014) took stock 25 years on: only a minority of people in the former socialist countries lived in clear economic success stories.",
  TF("Milanović concluded that nearly all post-socialist countries had converged on Western living standards by 2014.",false,"He found a minority of clear successes (such as Poland), with many countries barely back to their 1990 income levels.")],
 ["Surveys record real 'nostalgia' in parts of the former bloc, strongest among older and poorer respondents, usually for security and equality, not for censorship or one-party rule.",
  CASE("In a 2009 survey, a majority in several former Communist countries say ordinary people are worse off economically than under communism, while also approving of multi-party democracy.",M("How is this best interpreted?",["People can value security and freedom together","People want the old regime back entirely","The survey must be mistaken","People reject democracy"],"Pew's 2009 and 2019 surveys found this mix. Kristen Ghodsee and Mitchell Orenstein discuss it in *Taking Stock of Shock*."))],
 ["Ghodsee's work on women under state socialism notes real gains in employment, childcare and legal equality, alongside the 'double burden' of work and housework and, in Romania, coercive pro-natalism.",
  PAIR("Match the scholar to the argument.",[["Allen","Soviet growth was real; collectivization was not needed"],["Kornai","Shortage was systemic under classical socialism"],["Milanović","Transition produced few clear winners"],["Stiglitz","Institutions must come before privatization"]],"Each is a partial view, and they are compatible more often than their authors' polemics suggest.")],
 ["A balanced ledger therefore has entries that do not net out: fast early industrialization and broad basic welfare; mass repression, famine and unfreedom; stagnation; and a costly, uneven exit.",
  EX("Why is 'controlling for income level' important when comparing welfare outcomes under different systems, and what does it leave out?","Richer countries tend to have better health and education whatever their system, so a raw comparison of, say, the USSR with the United States mostly measures wealth. Comparing countries at similar income isolates what the system adds or subtracts. But it leaves out whether the system itself affects income growth over time, the reliability of official data, and things that are not counted in health and literacy figures at all, such as political freedom and repression.")]
]}]});

U({id:'grg',track:'gr',title:'Strategy now',scene:'news',blurb:'Power resources, non-reformist reforms and what the evidence says.',lessons:[
{t:'How reforms are won and kept',sub:'Power resources and their limits',items:[
 ["**Walter Korpi**'s **power resources theory**: the size and generosity of welfare states is explained by the organizational strength of labor: union density and left-party government.",
  DATA("Union density and collective bargaining coverage, about 2019",[["Sweden: density",65],["Sweden: coverage",88],["U.S.: density",10],["U.S.: coverage",12]],M("What does power resources theory predict from these figures?",["A more generous welfare state in Sweden","Identical social policy in both","Higher inequality in Sweden","No link between labor and policy"],"**Esping-Andersen** built on this to distinguish social democratic, conservative and liberal welfare regimes by how far they **decommodify** labor."),"Source: OECD")],
 ["**Decommodification** is the degree to which people can maintain a livelihood without relying on the market, for example through sick pay, pensions or unemployment benefits as of right.",
  SORT("More or less decommodifying?",{"More decommodifying":["Universal health care as a right","Unemployment pay near previous wage","Free public childcare"],"Less decommodifying":["Means-tested aid with work requirements","Health insurance tied to one's job"]},"Tying insurance to the job raises the cost of quitting or striking, which is why unions and employers both care about it.")],
 ["A rival view, **Varieties of Capitalism** (Hall and Soskice), holds that *employers* in 'coordinated market economies' supported social insurance because it protects investments in specific skills.",
  M("How does Varieties of Capitalism differ from power resources theory?",["It stresses employers' interests, not labor's strength","It denies that welfare states exist anywhere at all","It focuses only on agriculture","It predicts convergence on one model"],"If it is right, welfare states are less the spoils of class struggle than part of a production strategy.")],
 ["**André Gorz** distinguished **reformist reforms**, which fit within the system's logic, from **non-reformist reforms**, which shift power and open the way to further change.",
  CASE("Proposal A: a tax credit that tops up low wages. Proposal B: a law giving workers elected majorities on pension-fund boards that control large blocks of shares.",M("Which is closer to a 'non-reformist reform' in Gorz's sense, and why?",["B, because it shifts control over capital","A, because it raises incomes","A, because it costs more","Neither, since both are laws"],"A subsidizes employers' low wages and leaves power where it was. B alters who decides."))],
 ["The **Meidner Plan** was the clearest attempt. Its failure is read two ways: capital will fight socialization to the last (so reformism has a ceiling), or the plan was badly designed and had lukewarm backing from the Social Democrats and voters.",
  PICK("Which factors contributed to the wage-earner funds' defeat?",["A massive employer counter-mobilization","Lukewarm Social Democratic leadership","Weak public enthusiasm for union-run funds"],["A Soviet invasion threat","A constitutional ban on unions"],"On October 4, 1983, some 75,000 or more business supporters marched in Stockholm against the funds.")],
 ["Wright classified strategies as **ruptural** (break with the system), **interstitial** (build alternatives in the cracks) and **symbiotic** (reforms that also solve problems for elites).",
  PAIR("Match Wright's strategy type to an example.",[["Ruptural","A revolutionary seizure of power"],["Interstitial","Building worker co-ops and commons"],["Symbiotic","Social democratic class compromise"],["Eroding capitalism","Combining interstitial and symbiotic over time"]],"Wright favored 'eroding capitalism': taming it from above while building alternatives from below.")],
 ["**Vivek Chibber** and others argue for a **class-struggle social democracy**: use elections to win reforms that build working-class capacity, while expecting capital to resist. Critics on the left doubt the state can be used so; critics on the right doubt the economics.",
  TF("Chibber argues that cultural explanations, not material constraints, mainly account for workers' acceptance of capitalism.",false,"In *The Class Matrix* he argues the opposite: resignation flows from the real costs and risks of collective action, so strategy must lower those costs.")],
 ["So the live disagreements are about sequence and agency: which reforms compound into more power, and who can force them through.",
  EX("Explain Gorz's distinction between reformist and non-reformist reforms, with an example of each.","A reformist reform improves conditions in a way that fits the existing system's logic and leaves power relations as they are: for example a wage subsidy, or a tax credit. A non-reformist reform is judged by what it makes possible next: it shifts power toward workers or the public and weakens capital's control, so it creates a base for further demands. Examples are worker representation with real control over investment, taking a sector such as health out of the market altogether, or sectoral bargaining that strengthens unions' capacity.")]
]},
{t:'What the evidence says',sub:'Co-ops, public ownership and labor law',items:[
 ["Meta-analyses of employee ownership (for example O'Boyle and co-authors, 2016) find a small positive association with firm performance. Studies of worker co-ops (Pérotin; Burdín) find they survive at least as long as conventional firms.",
  M("What does the empirical literature generally find about worker co-ops' survival?",["They survive at least as well as other firms","They nearly always fail within their first two years","They cannot exist in markets","They always outgrow rivals"],"In Uruguay, Burdín found co-ops had a 29 percent lower hazard of dissolution than conventional firms.")],
 ["Then why are they rare? Explanations include difficulty raising equity without ceding control, risk concentration for members, founding costs, and the possibility that successful co-ops **degenerate** by hiring non-members.",
  SORT("Is this an explanation for why co-ops are rare, or evidence about how they perform?",{"Why they are rare":["Outside investors want control rights","Workers' wealth is undiversified","Few are founded in the first place"],"How they perform":["Similar or higher productivity","More stable employment in downturns"]},"The evidence suggests the bottleneck is formation, not performance.")],
 ["In recessions co-ops tend to cut pay and hours instead of jobs. Mondragon moved members between co-ops after 2008, and when **Fagor** failed in 2013 most members were relocated within the group.",
  CASE("Demand falls 20 percent. Firm A lays off a fifth of its staff. Firm B keeps everyone, cuts hours and pay by agreement, and transfers some members to sister firms.",M("Which is the typical co-op response?",["Firm B's","Firm A's","Neither one","Both equally often"],"Craig and Pencavel found this pattern in U.S. plywood co-ops as well."))],
 ["On **public ownership**, the evidence is mixed and sector-specific. Reviews of water and electricity find no systematic efficiency advantage for private operators. Hundreds of cities have **remunicipalized** water, including Paris in 2010.",
  TF("The research literature shows privately run water utilities are consistently more efficient than public ones.",false,"Meta-analyses (for example Bel and Warner) find no consistent cost advantage. Results depend on regulation and competition, not ownership alone.")],
 ["**The Preston model** (England) and **Cleveland's Evergreen Cooperatives** are examples of **community wealth building**: steering spending by 'anchor institutions' like hospitals and universities to local and worker-owned firms.",
  PICK("Which are elements of community wealth building?",["Local procurement by anchor institutions","Support for worker co-ops","Public or community banking"],["Tax breaks to attract outside corporations","Selling public land to the highest bidder"],"It is 'interstitial' in Wright's terms, and its scale so far is small.")],
 ["On **labor law**, economists increasingly find employers hold wage-setting (**monopsony**) power, and that moderate minimum wage rises have small employment effects (Card and Krueger; Cengiz and co-authors; Dube).",
  DATA("U.S. federal minimum wage in 2023 dollars",[["1968",14.0,"about $14.00"],["1990",8.9,"about $8.90"],["2009",10.3,"about $10.30"],["2023",7.25,"$7.25"]],M("What does the chart show?",["Its real value has fallen since 1968","It has kept pace with prices","It peaked in 2023","It doubled after 2009"],"The nominal rate has been $7.25 since 2009. Most U.S. workers now live in states or cities with higher minimums."),"Source: U.S. Department of Labor, adjusted with CPI")],
 ["Research on unions finds they compress wage differences, including racial gaps, and that union decline explains a meaningful share of rising U.S. wage inequality (Western and Rosenfeld estimate a fifth to a third).",
  CASE("Two similar countries: in one, bargaining is firm by firm and covers 12 percent of workers; in the other, it is industry-wide and covers 80 percent.",M("In which would you expect lower wage inequality, on the evidence?",["The one with industry-wide bargaining","The one with firm-level bargaining","There would be no difference","It cannot be studied"],"Sectoral bargaining takes wages out of competition between firms. Proposals for it have reached the U.S. debate through fast-food wage councils in California."))],
 ["Evidence disciplines both sides: co-ops and public firms are not doomed to inefficiency, and they are not automatically better. Design, governance and context decide.",
  EX("If worker co-ops perform about as well as conventional firms, why are there so few? Give the main explanations in your own words.","The problem seems to be getting started and growing, not surviving. Workers usually lack the savings to fund a firm, and outside investors want control in return for equity, which a co-op cannot give. Members would have both their job and their savings in one firm, which is risky. Banks and advisers are unfamiliar with the form. And when a co-op succeeds, members may be tempted to hire non-members or sell out. So few are created, even though those that exist are durable.")]
]}]});
